        "content": "The user is reporting that the app still shows a server startup error. The code now has venv setup and automatic dependency installation. The issue might be in how the venv is created or that the flask server fails to start for other reasons.\n\nLet me look at the full main.js carefully:\n\n1. Path for venv is `path.join(basePath, 'venv')` - but in dev mode, basePath is `filament-calculator/` which contains `requirements.txt`. Good.\n\n2. The execSync for venv creation uses backtick template with double quotes: `\"${venvDir}\"` - but the variable pythonCmd is not quoted in the first command. Actually line 226: `execSync(\\`\"${pythonCmd}\" -m venv \"${venvDir}\"\\`, ...)` - that looks fine.\n\n3. But there's a subtle bug: on line 226, the first template uses `\"${pythonCmd}\"` - but if pythonCmd is already quoted? It shouldn't be a problem.\n\n4. More importantly, on line 251: `execSync(\\`\"${pipPath}\" install -r \"${requirementsPath}\"\\`, ...)` - if requirements.txt doesn't exist, it falls through to install flask directly. But it also might fail if `pip` needs to be upgraded or if there's a network issue.\n\nHowever, the user already reported that the system Python works (3.14.5). The main issue might be that the `venv` module is not available in the system Python. Let me check that.\n\nAlso, the user said \"при запуске и обычной и appimage версии выдает ошибку запуска сервера\" - server startup error. This could be:\n- venv creation failure\n- dependency installation failure\n- server itself crashing after start\n\nLet me check if the system has venv available and try to trace the actual error.\n\nActually, there's another subtle issue: when `fs.existsSync(venvDir)` is false, the code tries to create venv. But on Linux, if you already have a `venv` directory (maybe from a previous failed attempt), the exists check returns true and it skips creation. Then it tries to use the broken venv.\n\nAlso, the venv creation command `\"${pythonCmd}\" -m venv \"${venvDir}\"` - if pythonCmd is \"python3\" (a command name, not a path), the double quotes around it will cause issues with execSync on Linux? Let me check: `\"python3\" -m venv \"path\"` should work fine in shell.\n\nBut more critically: if Python 3.14.5 is used and venv is not available in the system Python (some minimal installs don't include venv), the creation fails silently and returns, showing error.html.\n\nAlso, there's another bug: the `requirementsPath` variable on line 248 checks for `basePath/requirements.txt`. But in packaged mode, the filament-calculator directory is unpacked as `app.asar.unpacked/filament-calculator`, which should have the requirements.txt. However, in dev mode, basePath is `__dirname/filament-calculator` which should also have it.\n\nWait, actually I just remembered: the user had `requirements.txt` in `filament-calculator/` because the earlier code mentioned that file. But let me verify it actually exists there.\n\nLet me do a quick check of the file structure and also run a test to see if the venv creation works.",
